    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65699,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> superficial cervicovaginal myofibroblastoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65700,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65701,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vagina (most commonly), cervix and vulva (less often)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65702,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients may present with a polyp or cystic mass; lesions may be incidental.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65703,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are rare and occur over a wide age range (23&ndash;80&nbsp;years).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65704,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65705,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Superficial myofibroblastoma is thought to arise from subepithelial, hormonally sensitive, mesenchymal tissue of the lower genital tract. It is occasionally described in women taking tamoxifen {15693885}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65706,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Superficial myofibroblastoma is typically well circumscribed, nodular, or polypoid; generally &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm; and solid, with a uniform, firm cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65707,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Superficial myofibroblastoma is a well-circumscribed but unencapsulated lesion separated from epithelium by a grenz zone. It is moderately cellular and lacks defining architectural patterns, but lace-like, sieve-like, and fascicular patterns may be seen. There are uniform, bland stellate or spindle cells with a wavy nucleus and uniform chromatin; small multinucleate cells may be seen. There are few or no mitoses. The stroma is finely collagenous, with bands of thicker collagen but no entrapped fat {21820148; 15693885}. Spindle cells are positive for vimentin, CD34, ER, PR, and desmin, but negative for S100.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65708,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 420,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:22:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
